# No compatibility layers, no extra abstractions

Observer has not been released. There are no external callers, on-disk formats, or wire protocols that must keep working. Change the code to the shape it should have.

## Do not keep a compatibility layer

When a type, function, format, config key, or protocol field changes, update every caller and delete the old path in the same change.

- Do not add shims, aliases, `#[deprecated]` wrappers, versioned codecs, feature flags, or dual read/write paths so old and new code coexist.
- Do not preserve an old on-disk layout, frame, or API "just in case." Replace it.
- Tests should cover the current behavior only.

```rust
// Bad: old constructor kept so nothing else has to move
pub fn open(path: &Path) -> Result<Wal> {
    Self::open_v2(path, WalOptions::default())
}

// Good: one constructor, callers updated
pub fn open(path: &Path, options: WalOptions) -> Result<Wal> { /* ... */ }
```

## Do not add an abstraction you do not need

Prefer a concrete type and a straight function. Add a trait, generic, newtype, or module split only when a second implementation exists or the split removes real duplication.

- One implementor means no trait.
- A parameter or a private function is enough when only call-site configuration changes.
- Do not introduce a wrapper whose only job is to forward to the type it holds.

```rust
// Bad: trait with a single impl, plus a pass-through wrapper
trait MemtableStore { fn insert(&mut self, key: Key, value: Value); }
struct MemtableStoreImpl(Memtable);
impl MemtableStore for MemtableStoreImpl {
    fn insert(&mut self, key: Key, value: Value) { self.0.insert(key, value); }
}

// Good: call Memtable directly
memtable.insert(key, value);
```
